The correct answer is '**Option 2 (Because "system" is a singular subject)**'.

**Key Points**

***Option 1: Because "training" is the object***

This is **incorrect**. The verb form is determined by the subject, not the object. &ldquo;Training&rdquo; is the object of the verb &ldquo;focuses.&rdquo;

***Option 2: Because "system" is a singular subject***

This is **correct**. The subject of the sentence is &ldquo;The Japanese education system,&rdquo; which is singular. Therefore, the verb must agree in the singular form: &ldquo;focuses.&rdquo;

***Option 3: Because "education" is uncountable***

This is **incorrect**. While &ldquo;education&rdquo; is uncountable, the subject is &ldquo;system,&rdquo; not &ldquo;education.&rdquo; The verb agreement depends on &ldquo;system.&rdquo;

***Option 4: Because the sentence is in passive voice***

This is **incorrect**. The sentence is in active voice (&ldquo;system focuses&rdquo;), not passive. Passive would be &ldquo;is focused on.&rdquo;

Therefore, the correct answer is **Option 2**: *Because "system" is a singular subject.*

**Additional Information**

Subject-verb agreement rule: A singular subject takes a singular verb form (with -s or -es in present tense).
Example: &ldquo;The committee **decides** the rules.&rdquo; / &ldquo;The system **works** efficiently.&rdquo;
If the subject were plural (&ldquo;systems&rdquo;), the verb would be &ldquo;focus.&rdquo;
